Actually the orthogonal movement keys come from the vi editor, so they
predate any (t)hrow, (u)ntrap or other non-movement (net)hack commands..
and there is also another logic connecting some of them to the ascii
control codes:
^H is the backspace code that moves the cursor left.
^J is linefeed, which moves the cursor down.
I suspect that may be the reason why those keys were chosen for vi,
and then the author of the editor thought it would be logical to use the
next two keys to the right for moving up and right.

